// Change: new rule
// Title: PUA - NSSM Execution
// Sigma ID: 5ded8ee2-26b0-4702-be05-29e32694a5e0
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Swachchhanda Shrawan Poudel (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2026-10-02 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: execution, persistence, privilege-escalation, T1543.003
// Description: Detects execution of PUA 'NSSM' (the Non-Sucking Service Manager) which is a legitimate tool for
//   managing Windows services, but is often abused by threat actors to maintain persistence by
//   installing malicious payloads as system services.
// False positives:
//   - NSSM is legitimately used by several applications
//   - Administrators using NSSM for legitimate service management
// References:
//   - https://dfir.ch/posts/field_notes_nssm/
//   - https://thedfirreport.com/2020/04/20/sqlserver-or-the-miner-in-the-basement/
//   - https://www.sophos.com/en-us/blog/sharpening-the-knife-gold-blades-strategic-evolution
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/77b8c5723f38e4e8cc448d499ad3dabd08f47406/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_pua_nssm.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\nssm.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoFileDescription =~ "The non-sucking service manager" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "nssm.exe") and (not((FolderPath startswith "C:\\Program Files\\" or FolderPath startswith "C:\\Program Files (x86)\\")))
